---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeFancyText from '@/components/tools/text/DevEncodeFancyText.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="small-text-generator">
  <DevEncodeFancyText
    client:load
    sample="small text"
    featured={['small-caps', 'superscript', 'subscript']}
  />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Three kinds of small text</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Style</th><th>Sample</th><th>Sits</th><th>Coverage</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Small caps</td><td>sᴍᴀʟʟ ᴄᴀᴘs</td><td>On the baseline</td><td>All letters except X</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Superscript</td><td>ˢᵘᵖᵉʳˢᶜʳᶦᵖᵗ</td><td>Raised</td><td>All letters except Q; all digits</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Subscript</td><td>ₛᵤbₛcᵣᵢₚₜ</td><td>Lowered</td><td>Only about half the alphabet; all digits</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Small caps are the most usable of the three: small capitals that stay on the baseline, so a whole
      phrase still reads as text. Superscript is much smaller and raised. It suits a short marker but is hard
      to read past a few words. Subscript has the worst coverage, because Unicode added only the letters used
      in chemical and mathematical notation.
    </p>

    <h2>Why some letters are missing</h2>
    <p>
      These characters were not designed as a styling set. Unicode added them for phonetics, mathematics and
      chemistry, so it has only the letters those fields needed. There is no small-caps X, no superscript Q,
      and no subscript for most consonants. The small text generator shows a missing character at full size,
      and you can see it in the preview.
    </p>

    <h2>Good uses for small text</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A quiet subtitle under a profile name or a channel description.</li>
      <li>Footnote markers: superscript digits are complete, so ¹ ² ³ work.</li>
      <li>Chemical formulae in plain text: H₂O, CO₂, C₆H₁₂O₆.</li>
      <li>Maths in a bio: x², 10⁻⁹, n^th written as nᵗʰ.</li>
      <li>Visual separation in a Discord or Twitch panel that has no formatting.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Do not use it for disclaimers</h2>
    <p>
      Do not use small text for anything a reader has a right to see. It fails on devices without the glyphs,
      screen readers read it unpredictably or skip it, and in several jurisdictions contract terms made hard
      to read on purpose are unenforceable. If the content matters, use ordinary text.
    </p>

    <h2>Usernames and display names</h2>
    <p>
      Support varies. Discord and Twitch generally accept these characters in display names and channel
      panels. Twitter/X accepts them in the display name but not the handle. Instagram accepts them in the bio
      and the name field but sometimes rejects them in the username. Many platforms allow only ASCII in
      handles. Test first, because some services make you wait before you can change a name back.
    </p>

    <h2>Superscript versus a real exponent</h2>
    <p>
      In a document with formatting, such as Word, Google Docs or HTML, use the real superscript command. It
      scales with the font, assistive technology reads it correctly, and it copies cleanly into equation
      editors. Use Unicode superscript only where no formatting is available.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
